@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+# Opt-in pipeline snapshot prototype (not deployed)
+
+Based on official c50ef849a541cd2a826167b9494fa7d0d7d134b4 (v0.6.0).
+Absent/disabled `pipeline_snapshot` preserves upstream scheduling, metrics and RPC.
+Enabled mode bypasses that scheduler, Redis and metric store entirely. No durable
+receipt/store migration is involved. It uses the pinned upstream client-go API
+client, with verified TLS, no environment proxy, no redirects, same-origin GET
+boundary, 1 MiB response bound, no HTTP retries and a whole-poll deadline.
+The existing gitlab.maximum_requests_per_second and burstable_requests_per_second
+settings are honored (each 1..10), shared across all snapshot requests. Rate waiting
+counts against the whole-poll deadline. Unknown snapshot/target YAML keys fail.
+No new API scopes or real credentials are created by this prototype.
+
+Example addition (synthetic IDs, not a deployable Portal selection):
+
+```yaml
+pipeline_snapshot:
+  enabled: true
+  interval_seconds: 30
+  timeout_seconds: 10
+  targets:
+    - project_id: 7
+      project: tenants/portal/proof
+      ref: main
+      source: push
+```
+
+Existing `projects` must exactly match distinct target paths. Explicitly disable
+jobs, variables, reports, environments, tags, MRs, webhook, pprof, Redis, wildcard
+and OpenTelemetry export. Legacy pull/garbage-collect schedules are not started in
+this mode. Only push/branch is supported, at most 16 unique targets, literal
+bounded refs, consistent project ID/path mappings. Sum of target deadlines must
+fit one interval. A sequential cycle waits interval_seconds after its work, so the
+interval is not a sampling SLA. API credentials remain project-wide read authority,
+not ref-level authority. Only configured labels are emitted.
+
+Every observation reads project ID/path, exact branch existence, latest branch-scoped pipeline
+list ordered by ID descending for exact ref/source (per_page=1), and selected
+pipeline detail. Detail tag=true is rejected even for a same-name branch/tag. All identity fields are rechecked; no next-page traversal is
+needed for this latest-only query. The GitLab list/detail sequence is not a native
+transaction: a pipeline can change or a newer one appear during the observation.
+An empty list is an observed absence, never a synthetic pipeline success. Foreign
+IDs/refs/sources, absent fields, auth/HTTP/TLS/timeout/parse errors fail closed.
+
+Metrics prefix `gcpe_pipeline_snapshot_`:
+- `valid`: 0 at cold start, refresh start and failure; 1 only after complete success;
+  also becomes 0 at scrape if age exceeds 3 intervals or wall age is negative/over that ceiling.
+- `last_success_timestamp_seconds`: local completion time, 0 before first success.
+- `observation_age_seconds`: monotonic elapsed time when available, -1 initially.
+- `present`: whether the last complete result contained a pipeline.
+- `generation`: per-target poll generation, reset on restart.
+- `status{status="failed|success|other"}`: historical status, three fixed series.
+
+All metrics share static project_id/project/ref/source labels. Consumers MUST gate
+status on valid, present and bounded age. A failed observation retains historical
+status but cannot authorize recovery. Fresh running/canceled/unknown and absence
+must produce NoData/KeepLast for the incident, not a zero interpreted as recovery.
+Use a separate freshness/invalid-observation alert. Server /health endpoints in
+this mode are process health ONLY, not evidence of successful API polling.
+
+Per-target mutex serializes polls; one short collector mutex copies/publishes
+complete observations without blocking across network calls. No partial metric
+writes exist: local candidate becomes visible only through one in-memory assignment.
+There is therefore no persistence-success metric or store-error swallowing path.
+Configured state is immutable; restart/reconfiguration requires fresh observations,
+with no persisted freshness. Multiple processes/Redis/HA are unsupported.
+
+Clock bounds: Go monotonic ages protect existing process observations against wall
+clock jumps; the independent wall-age bound rejects suspend-aged or backwards/future stamps. Both elapsed clocks also bound poll completion. A
+clock jump does not prove source freshness. Incident rules should prefer monotonic
+observation_age_seconds, not merely time()-last_success_timestamp_seconds.
+
+Tests use fake TLS servers and fake credentials only: lifecycle, concurrent scrapes,
+serialized polls, timeouts/cancellation, TLS/redirect refusal, mismatched identities,
+malformed/oversize responses, clock rollback/staleness, restart and unsupported
+modes. No test establishes native GitLab19.4.2 permissions or real delivery.
+
+This opt-in fork requires independently reviewed source, reproducible build,
+immutable artifact/image provenance and upgrade regression maintenance. No image
+has been built/published, and no service, token, project or notification deployed.
